  "id": "munich_eurodisco",
  "name": "Munich sound (1970s Eurodisco)",
  "family": "electronic",
  "lineage": "Disco made in West Germany from the mid-1970s, centred on Munich, where Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte produced at Musicland Studios and Michael Kunze and Sylvester Levay created Silver Convention, whose 'Fly, Robin, Fly' topped the US chart in 1975. Producer-led studio teams built records from session rhythm sections, string and horn charts and stacked choruses, and Frank Farian's Boney M., on the Hansa label, carried West German Eurodisco across Europe.",
  "recipe": "classic European munich sound, electronic Dance, continental branch. single lead single track no stacking. late 1970s thirds fifths octaves 16 24 track multitrack vocal stack, drum kit, mid rich alder electric bass, credited strings unspecified, alder rosewood single coil electric guitar, analog mono synth. treated studio. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, Pultec passive program EQ. Studer 30ips tape echo.",
